speechtext:
Mr. Speaker, there are two orders of reference with which I want to deal at this point in time, on the basis of consent. There have been consultations with the opposition Parties and if Your Honour were to put these motions forward-they are references-you will find that there is unanimous consent. The first order of reference is to the Standing Committee on Finance, Trade and Economic Affairs and reads as follows:
That the document entitled "The Regulation of Canadian Financial Institutions: Proposals for Discussion," tabled earlier this day, be referred to the Standing Committee on Finance, Trade and Economic Affairs;
That the Committee examine and report on the proposals contained therein;
That the Committee be empowered to retain expert, clerical and secretarial staff as may be deemed necessary; and
That the Committee submit its final report to the House of Commons no later than September 30, 1985.
That reference has received the assent of all Parties and if you put it, Mr. Speaker, I think it would be adopted unanimously.
